WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.400 --> 00:00:06.960
What happens to a photoreceptor if you cut its calcium feedback?

2
00:00:08.083 --> 00:00:10.303
Calcium enters through the open channels.

3
00:00:11.123 --> 00:00:13.003
An exchanger pumps it out all the time.

4
00:00:13.967 --> 00:00:18.267
Predict. Light closes the channels, and pumping goes on.

5
00:00:19.087 --> 00:00:20.427
Which way does calcium move?

6
00:00:26.417 --> 00:00:30.357
Calcium falls. Entry drops, while pumping continues.

7
00:00:31.450 --> 00:00:33.430
Two main calcium sensors answer.

8
00:00:34.650 --> 00:00:38.730
First, the GCAPs speed cGMP synthesis, up to about twentyfold.

9
00:00:39.783 --> 00:00:41.883
More cGMP reopens channels.

10
00:00:42.903 --> 00:00:45.263
This is the strongest of the calcium feedbacks.

11
00:00:46.083 --> 00:00:48.883
Second, recoverin lets go of GRK1.

12
00:00:49.723 --> 00:00:53.423
That kinase switches off active pigment, so pigment shuts off sooner.

13
00:00:54.467 --> 00:00:56.247
One feedback does two jobs.

14
00:00:56.247 --> 00:00:59.347
On a steady background, it reopens channels.

15
00:00:59.347 --> 00:01:01.367
That keeps current in reserve.

16
00:01:02.450 --> 00:01:05.250
During each new flash, it pushes against the change.

17
00:01:06.170 --> 00:01:08.570
Each photon closes less current, for less time.

18
00:01:09.533 --> 00:01:14.933
Now cut the loop. Mendez and colleagues studied mouse rods lacking both GCAPs.

19
00:01:15.983 --> 00:01:21.023
Predict. In darkness, is a single photon's response bigger or smaller?

20
00:01:22.003 --> 00:01:24.643
On a background, does the rod fail sooner or later?

21
00:01:30.717 --> 00:01:33.117
In darkness, about five times bigger.

22
00:01:33.977 --> 00:01:39.957
Without the GCAPs, cGMP is restored more slowly, so responses recover far more

23
00:01:39.957 --> 00:01:40.957
slowly.

24
00:01:41.400 --> 00:01:44.580
On a background, adaptation was severely compromised.

25
00:01:45.683 --> 00:01:51.203
In cones lacking the GCAPs and recoverin, the fitted half-sensitivity point sat at

26
00:01:51.203 --> 00:01:52.283
about a quarter of normal.

27
00:01:53.163 --> 00:01:55.383
That shift was not statistically significant.

28
00:01:56.517 --> 00:02:01.017
But their sensitivity fell more steeply as the background brightened, and that

29
00:02:01.017 --> 00:02:02.057
difference was significant.

30
00:02:03.167 --> 00:02:07.927
So removing the feedback makes rod responses larger in the dark, and adaptation

31
00:02:07.927 --> 00:02:09.127
worse on a background.

32
00:02:10.267 --> 00:02:13.407
In darkness, only the trimming of each response can show.

33
00:02:14.267 --> 00:02:16.787
On a background, the reserve comes into play too.

34
00:02:17.800 --> 00:02:18.960
Now the arithmetic.

35
00:02:20.020 --> 00:02:22.500
Take a small, brief test flash of fixed duration.

36
00:02:23.460 --> 00:02:28.720
Its extra intensity is contrast times background, and its photon count is that times

37
00:02:28.720 --> 00:02:33.940
the duration. Its peak response is about sensitivity times that photon count.

38
00:02:34.933 --> 00:02:37.273
Multiply sensitivity by background.

39
00:02:38.153 --> 00:02:40.693
For normal cones, the product climbs to a ceiling.

40
00:02:41.613 --> 00:02:44.333
On bright backgrounds, contrast responses hold.

41
00:02:47.333 --> 00:02:52.253
For the knockout-cone fit, well above the half-sensitivity background, the product

42
00:02:52.253 --> 00:02:54.933
falls about threefold for each tenfold brightening.

43
00:02:57.933 --> 00:03:01.773
A fall steeper than Weber's rule fits a cell running short of current.

44
00:03:02.513 --> 00:03:04.713
But the slope alone cannot measure the current.

45
00:03:05.717 --> 00:03:07.777
Some adaptation survives the cut.

46
00:03:08.577 --> 00:03:10.037
What supplies it is still unresolved.

47
00:03:11.200 --> 00:03:13.720
Rods in bright light raise the same question.

48
00:03:14.600 --> 00:03:18.280
On a very bright background, mouse rod-driven responses dip at first.

49
00:03:19.333 --> 00:03:21.773
Then they recover with continued exposure.

50
00:03:22.653 --> 00:03:26.213
A model suggests that gain changes and pigment bleaching help.

51
00:03:27.167 --> 00:03:30.807
So in these mice, rods do not simply switch off in bright light.

52
00:03:31.800 --> 00:03:36.740
The takeaway. The feedback trims each response and keeps current in reserve.

53
00:03:37.760 --> 00:03:39.340
Cut it, and the cell runs short sooner.
